   "correctedAnswer": "The motives to any business for holding cash are: 1. Transaction Motive: This refers to the cash required by a firm to meet its day-to-day needs, such as making payments for salaries, wages, interests, dividends, and goods purchased, as well as receiving cash from sales, debtors, and investments. 2. Precautionary Motive: This refers to the tendency of a firm to hold cash to meet unforeseen circumstances or contingencies that may arise in the course of business, such as changes in raw material prices, labor strikes, lockouts, or changes in demand. By holding cash, firms can ensure an uninterrupted flow of business operations and minimize the risk of financial difficulties."
